<Project>

  <!--
    Centralized build settings. Values defined here apply to every project in
    the solution; individual .csproj files only override when they need to
    diverge. Keeps the "best-practice baseline" in one place.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest</AnalysisLevel>
    <AnalysisMode>All</AnalysisMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Generate XML documentation by default. Test projects opt out by
    setting <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    in their csproj. (We can't gate this on $(IsPackable) here because
    the SDK locks in its decision before Directory.Build.targets runs.)
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Lock-file based version pinning. The lock file (packages.lock.json)
    captures exactly the resolved package versions and is committed to git,
    so any clone restores the same dependency graph. RestoreLockedMode fails
    the restore if the lock file is out of sync with the csproj.

    Unconditional, deliberately. This was gated on ContinuousIntegrationBuild,
    under a comment reading "only enabled in CI so local `dotnet add package`
    keeps working". No ref in this repository's history has ever contained a
    .github directory or any .yml or .yaml file, so there was no CI for the
    gate to be enabled in: the sentence named a tradeoff between local
    convenience and a CI guarantee where only the convenience ever existed.
    The gate was not dormant pending a workflow, it was never on anywhere,
    and the comment implied otherwise. Nor does the SDK infer the property
    from a CI environment: measured on 10.0.400 in two sibling members, it
    evaluates empty even with CI and GITHUB_ACTIONS set.

    A file whose promise depends on a switch set somewhere else cannot state
    its own promise. Removing the condition makes the promise this file's
    own, which is the reason to prefer it over waiting for a workflow.

    What it buys here, stated precisely because it is less than it sounds.
    The guarantee reaches the package class only: Collatz.Tests' four test
    packages, coverlet.collector, Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk, xunit and
    xunit.runner.visualstudio. It does not reach the BigRational edge, which
    this member takes by ProjectReference. A version arriving that way is
    recorded as a constraint in the consuming project's dependencies map and
    is not validated. Proven rather than inferred: RationalApproximation has
    locked mode live in CI and ran green with a stale preview1 constraint
    against a preview2 producer (halheinrich/Math#44). ContinuedFractions is
    the inverse case; it consumes BigRational as a package, so there the
    change does protect that edge.

    Accepted cost: a deliberate package change now fails the restore with
    NU1004 instead of quietly rewriting the lock. Recover with
    `dotnet restore -p:RestoreForceEvaluate=true`, which is what the CLI's
    force-evaluate switch sets, and commit the regenerated lock. One command,
    once per intended change, on an artefact whose whole purpose is that it
    does not change silently. (The switch is spelled here as a property
    because an XML comment may not contain a double hyphen.) NuGet writes the
    regenerated lock with CRLF while .gitattributes pins it to LF, so
    `git status` then reports a change that `git diff` cannot show.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
    <RestoreLockedMode>true</RestoreLockedMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
